# s15b_regen_density.sh — WRITTEN, RUN, AND WRONG. Kept as a record, not as a tool.
#
# WHAT IT TRIED
# To write standalone .scfp density files for orca_vpot by rerunning each in vacuo
# single point with MORead + NoIter + KeepDens, i.e. reading the converged orbitals and
# doing zero SCF iterations.
#
# WHY IT FAILED
# All 44 frames were rejected by the script's own energy check: the zero-iteration job
# returned -0.056 Eh against a committed -836.118 Eh. The orbitals were not being read;
# the job was not computing the molecule at all. The check did its work - every frame
# was refused rather than writing a garbage density that would have poisoned the entire
# difference-potential map with nothing downstream able to detect it.
#
# WHY IT WAS UNNECESSARY IN THE FIRST PLACE
# ORCA 6 keeps the SCF density INSIDE the .densities container rather than as a loose
# .scfp file, and orca_vpot's SECOND ARGUMENT IS THE DENSITY'S NAME IN THAT CONTAINER,
# not a path on disk. The ORCA 6.0 manual says so obliquely: "In the case that basename
# of gbw- and densities-file do not match, you have to pass the densities' name as fifth
# argument to orca_vpot." A fifth argument for the container name only makes sense if
# the second is a container entry.
#
# Verified 2026-09-30 on sp_24883_R:
#     orca_vpot sp_24883_R.gbw sp_24883_R.scfp points.xyz out.txt
#   -> "Electrostatic potential evaluated in 0.428 sec", four columns, potential last.
# No .scfp file exists on disk and none is needed. s15_dv_matrix.py calls it this way.
#
# THE LESSON WORTH KEEPING
# Four separate routes were attempted before the manual was read closely enough:
# passing .densities directly, KeepDens on a fresh single point, MORead/NoIter
# regeneration, and orca_plot extraction. Each was plausible and none was checked
# against a one-line test first. The one-line test took two minutes and settled it.
